      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Whisk the rice flour, tapioca starch, water, and 1 tbsp vegetable oil until smooth. Leave to rest for 30 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fry the shallots in 1 tbsp oil until softened. Add the pork mince and mushrooms. Cook over medium heat for 5 to 6 minutes until cooked through. Add the fish sauce and sugar. Taste and adjust. Set aside to cool slightly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the dipping sauce by combining the fish sauce, lime juice, and sugar with 4 tbsp of water. Stir until dissolved."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat a 26 cm non-stick frying pan over medium heat. Brush lightly with oil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour in enough batter to thinly coat the base of the pan. Swirl immediately. Cover with a lid and cook for 1 to 2 minutes until the sheet is set and translucent."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spoon a thin line of filling along one edge of the sheet. Roll the sheet up using a spatula and slide onto a plate. Repeat with the remaining batter and filling."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve the rolls drizzled with crispy shallot oil if you have it. Add fresh coriander, bean sprouts, and the dipping sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The rice sheet tears when rolling.",
              "cause": "The batter was too thin or the sheet was not cooked enough.",
              "fix": "Add an extra tablespoon of rice flour to thicken the batter slightly. Cook until fully set and slightly pulling away from the pan before rolling."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The sheet sticks to the pan.",
              "cause": "The pan was not oiled enough or the batter was too starchy.",
              "fix": "Use a good non-stick pan and brush with oil between each sheet. Ensure the batter has rested for 30 minutes before cooking."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "The rolls are gummy and dense instead of light and silky.",
              "cause": "The batter was too thick.",
              "fix": "Thin the batter with a little more water until it is the consistency of very thin cream. The sheet should be almost transparent."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bánh cuốn is a northern Vietnamese dish most closely linked to Hanoi. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It is a classic breakfast food, eaten at small street stalls in the early morning. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The town of Thanh Trì near Hanoi is especially famous for its bánh cuốn. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "It takes skill and practice to steam the sheets thin enough. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In Hanoi, whole families specialise in making this dish and pass the skill down through generations."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
